When `ret.Push(NewPair(key, val))` is called, it might trigger a reallocation of the internal storage of the `linkedPairs` structure. While the `linkedPairs` struct itself doesn't change address, any pointers to specific elements within it (like individual `Pair` objects) could become invalid if the underlying storage is reallocated.

However, looking at the immediate context, I don't see any code that would be holding references to specific `Pair` objects within `ret` before the `Push` call. 

But let me think about this differently. The issue might be in how the `linkedPairs` structure manages its memory. Looking at the `set` method in `buffer.go`:

```go
func (self *linkedPairs) set(i int, v Pair) {
    if i < _DEFAULT_NODE_CAP {
        self.head[i] = v
        if self.size <= i {
            self.size = i+1
        }
        return
    }
    a, b := i/_DEFAULT_NODE_CAP-1, i%_DEFAULT_NODE_CAP
    if a < 0 {
        self.head[b] = v
    } else {
        self.growTailLength(a+1)
        var n = &self.tail[a]
        if *n == nil {
            *n = new(pairChunk)
        }
        (*n)[b] = v
    }
    if self.size <= i {
        self.size = i+1
    }
}
```

And `growTailLength`:
```go
func (self *linkedPairs) growTailLength(l int) {
    if l <= len(self.tail) {
        return
    }
    c := cap(self.tail)
    for c < l {
        c += 1 + c>>_APPEND_GROW_SHIFT
    }
    if c == cap(self.tail) {
        self.tail = self.tail[:l]
        return
    }
    tmp := make([]*pairChunk, l, c)
    copy(tmp, self.tail)
    self.tail = tmp
}
```

The issue is in the `growTailLength` method. When the `tail` slice needs to grow, it calls `make([]*pairChunk, l, c)` which creates a new slice with a different underlying array. This means that any pointers to elements in the old `tail` array would become invalid.

